Description: Add keyboard accessibility options (sticky, slow, bounce and mouse keys)
 sway has no equivalent of the X server's AccessX features, so the
 desktop's Typing accessibility settings do nothing in the Wayland
 session while they work in the X11 session. This adds per-keyboard
 input options, settable in the config file and at run time with
 swaymsg:
 .
   sticky_keys enabled|disabled
   sticky_keys_two_key_off enabled|disabled
   slow_keys <ms>                 (0 disables)
   bounce_keys <ms>               (0 disables)
   mouse_keys enabled|disabled
   mouse_keys_init_delay <ms>
   mouse_keys_accel_time <ms>
   mouse_keys_max_speed <px/s>
 .
 Sticky keys latch a tapped modifier for the next key and lock it when
 it is tapped twice; with sticky_keys_two_key_off, pressing two
 modifiers together releases them. Slow keys deliver a press only once
 the key has been held for the delay. Bounce keys drop a press of a key
 that comes within the delay after its previous accepted press. Mouse
 keys move the pointer with the keypad direction keys, accelerating
 linearly to the maximum speed (no button emulation yet).
 .
 The state is kept per keyboard, so keyboards with any of these options
 on are not grouped. With none of the options set, key events take the
 same path as before. The options are reported by get_inputs and
 documented in sway-input(5) and sway-ipc(7).
